4. Results
This section reports findings from 60 educator interviews (n = 30 per site), supplemented by 30 non-participant classroom observations conducted during an eight-month academic period. Interviews lasted 45–75 minutes and were analysed in MAXQDA (v.24) using the operational coding matrix in Section 3. Inter-coder agreement was assessed on 12 transcripts (20% of the corpus): Cohen’s κ increased from 0.82 in the initial iteration to 0.88 in the final round, indicating high agreement (Landis & Koch, 1977).
The results proceed from the comparative ideological distribution (§4.1) to within-site consensus and dispersion (§4.2), thematic distribution (§4.3), and lexical profile (§4.4).
4.1. Comparative Ideological Landscape: Quantitative Overview
The first research question asked whether educator ideologies diverge systematically between the two sites. Coding of all 60 transcripts into a single categorical label per participant — Standard Language Ideology (SLI), Inclusionary/Translanguaging, or Neutral/No Opinion — produced the cross-tabulation presented in Table 4.1. The distribution is not merely unequal; it is almost perfectly partitioned, with the two sites occupying nearly disjoint regions of the ideological space.
