Note. The first percentage reflects the share within the site (n = 30), while the second percentage reflects the share of the total aggregate sample (N = 60).
As Table 4.1 demonstrates, 80.0% of Sandton educators (n = 24) adhere to a Standard Language Ideology, whereas 100.0% of Soweto educators (n = 30) align with inclusionary translanguaging. The Sandton cohort divides its remaining third between inclusionary positions (n = 3) and a stance of neutral or resigned compliance (n = 3). The Soweto cohort records no SLI adherence and no neutral category at all: every participant converged on a pedagogical position that treats non-standard varieties as resources rather than deficits.
A Pearson chi-square test of independence confirmed a highly significant association between research site and ideological label, χ²(2) = 49.09, exact two-sided p < .001. The recomputed value of Cramér's V for this table is .90 (V = .9045 to four decimal places), computed as V = √(χ²/n ÷ min(k − 1, r − 1)) with χ² = 49.09, n = 60, and min(k − 1, r − 1) = 1; the statistic would equal exactly 49.0909, and the two-sided p-value under a chi-square distribution with two degrees of freedom is approximately 2.19 × 10⁻¹¹. By any conventional threshold this constitutes an exceptionally strong association between the site of instruction and the ideological orientation of the educator.
The strength of this association must, however, be read against its structural cause. Because the Soweto column for SLI and the Soweto column for Neutral/No Opinion are both zero, the marginal distribution itself is close to deterministic: knowing a participant's site almost entirely determines their label. This design property, discussed further in §4.2 and §6.3, means the association is best interpreted as evidence of institutional and material determination rather than as a measure of individual variability. Figure 1 plots the stance distribution across the two sites.
