The indices in Table 4.2 confirm that the Soweto cohort exhibits absolute ideological convergence (normalized Shannon entropy = 0.000; Simpson's index = 0.000), united in the necessity of multilingual pedagogical adaptation. Conversely, the Sandton cohort displays greater dispersion (normalized Shannon entropy = 0.582; Simpson's index = 0.340), accommodating minor proportions of inclusionary pedagogy (10.0%) and neutral or ambivalent stances (10.0%).
The asymmetry between these two values is analytically consequential. A maximum modal share of 1.000 with zero entropy indicates that, within the Soweto site, the commitment to multilingual pedagogical adaptation is not a preference held by a majority but an operational precondition recognized by every participant — a convergence consistent with the material constraints reported under MAT-LACK and CLASS-CROWD, where resources and class size eliminate the conditions under which monolingual correction could even be attempted. A modal share of 0.800 with an entropy of 0.582 in Sandton indicates a dominant norm with a live, if marginal, internal dissent: one educator in ten rejects the prestige-accent mandate and one in ten declines to take a position on it at all. The Sandton ideological field is therefore contested at its edges, whereas the Soweto field is settled.
