The thematic profile sharpens the finding reported in §4.1 in two respects. First, the normative theme dominates the Sandton corpus to a near-exclusive degree: 83.3% of Sandton interviews generated Standard/Prescriptive Ideology as their dominant theme, and only one participant (3.3%) produced Structural/Material Constraints. In Soweto, that normative theme does not appear once. Second, the three non-normative themes cluster overwhelmingly in the township site: Inclusionary Translanguaging accounts for 63.3% of Soweto interviews against 10.0% in Sandton, while Structural/Material Constraints (16.7% against 3.3%) and Affective Ecology and Filter (20.0% against 3.3%) together account for more than a third of the Soweto corpus and under a tenth of the Sandton corpus.
The thematic coding is grounded in distinctive lexical choices that recur across the coded extracts. Sandton participants frame competence through institutional and professional gatekeeping — S-01 observes that "students must master a standard accent for professional success," S-02 that she corrects "every grammar mistake to ensure they sound academic," and S-21 that "a polished accent opens doors that a local one might keep closed." Compliance is frequently framed as procedural: S-05 states, "I just follow the CAPS curriculum and the school's guidelines." In Soweto, by contrast, translanguaging is described as an unavoidable condition of instruction rather than a methodological option — SW-11 states that "translanguaging is not a choice; it is a necessity for survival" — and material scarcity enters the account directly, as when SW-12 notes that "we share one book between three students; we have bigger problems." Affective consequences appear on both sides of the divide but are localized differently: Sandton educators describe student silence as a response to peer judgment (S-14: "they remain silent because they fear peer mockery over non-standard pronunciation"), while Soweto educators describe it as a response to the withdrawal of corrective pressure (SW-07: "most of my students are terrified of making mistakes in English"; SW-18: "if I correct every error, they will stop talking altogether").
4.4. Lexical Profile of Interview Extracts
A lexical analysis of the raw interview extracts provides insight into the primary discursive preoccupations of educators in each environment. Content words were counted after the removal of stopwords, so that the resulting lists reflect the substantive vocabulary through which each cohort articulates its professional practice.
Sandton cohort: total tokens and mean extract length are reported in the analysis output accompanying this section. The most frequent content words (excluding stopwords) were: standard, accent, english, students, follow, precision, professional, and correct.
Soweto cohort: total tokens and mean extract length are likewise reported in the accompanying analysis output. The most frequent content words were: english, use, accent, translanguaging, students, zulu, correct, and bsae.
Three features of this comparison warrant comment. First, both cohorts place english and accent among their most frequent content words, confirming that the object of contention is shared even where the evaluative stance toward it is not: the disagreement concerns the status to be assigned to accent, not whether accent matters. Second, the surrounding vocabulary diverges sharply in kind. The Sandton list is composed of normative and institutional markers — standard, precision, professional, follow — which position accent as a property to be certified and reproduced. The Soweto list is composed of practical action markers — use, translanguaging, zulu — which position language as an instrument to be deployed for comprehension. The term bsae, a local designation for the varieties used in everyday township communication, appears in the Soweto list but not in the Sandton list, as does translanguaging itself. Third, the presence of correct on both lists indicates that corrective practice is not simply the monopoly of the elite site; what differs is its object — phonetic precision in Sandton, comprehensibility and participation in Soweto.
The lexical shift from normative markers (standard, precision, professional) in Sandton to practical action markers (use, translanguaging, zulu) in Soweto reinforces the qualitative divergence between elite linguistic gatekeeping and township pedagogical survival.
Reporting Notes on the Quantitative Values
The quantitative claims in this section were verified against the cross-tabulation in Table 4.1 before being reported, and the recomputation is recorded here for transparency and for reviewer audit.
With the observed table (Sandton: 24 / 3 / 3; Soweto: 0 / 30 / 0) and expected frequencies of 12.0, 16.5, and 1.5 in each row, the per-cell contributions to Pearson's statistic sum to 49.0909. This reproduces the reported test result χ²(2) = 49.09 and confirms that the reported p-value is below any conventional alpha: the exact two-sided p-value for two degrees of freedom is approximately 2.19 × 10⁻¹¹, given that the survival function of the chi-square distribution with df = 2 is exp(−x/2).
The effect size requires more careful statement than the test statistic. Following the standard definition, Cramér's V is the square root of the chi-square statistic divided by the product of the sample size and the smaller of (k − 1) and (r − 1); for this table V = √(49.0909 / (60 × 1)) = .9045, which rounds to .90 rather than to the .91 recorded in the earlier draft. Two further considerations bear on the figure finally reported. First, Cramér's V is a biased estimator that tends to overestimate the strength of association, and a bias-corrected variant (Bergsma, 2013) is available that subtracts the expected value under independence, (k − 1)(r − 1) / (n − 1), from the squared phi coefficient before rescaling; applied to this table it yields V = .886. Second, because the Soweto row contains two structurally empty cells, the association is constrained by design, and any effect size computed from a table of this shape approaches its upper bound by construction.
Accordingly, the manuscript should report the effect size as V = .90 (bias-corrected V = .89), and any discussion of its magnitude should be accompanied by the caveat in §6.3 concerning the zero-cell structure. The following values remain to be inserted from the analysis output before submission, as they are held as equation objects in the source file and did not survive extraction: the exact p-value as printed to three decimal places or in exponential form in the text and in the Table 4.1 and 4.2 captions; the total token counts and mean extract lengths for both cohorts in §4.4; the degrees-of-freedom figure in the §4.1 test sentence; and the entropy and Simpson values if they are to be repeated inline rather than cited to Table 4.2. Two further editorial corrections belong to this pass: the two figures currently labelled "Figure 1" (at the conceptual framework stage and within §4.1) require distinct numbering, and the duplicated sentence beginning "As demonstrated in Table 4.1…" appears three times across the document and should be retained only in §4.1.
